### Frontend Development Rules

#### When to Check These Rules
- When working on any frontend code
- When creating or modifying components
- When implementing new UI features
- When working with templates
- When adding or modifying styles

#### Must-Check Items
- [ ] Using correct icon implementation
- [ ] Following TailwindCSS conventions
- [ ] Using appropriate component structure
- [ ] Template logic is properly organized
- [ ] Styles follow utility-first approach

#### Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Using raw SVGs instead of humicon helper
- Creating new components when existing ones suffice
- Mixing presentation and business logic
- Using inline styles
- Duplicating component functionality

#### Visual Elements

##### Icons
- Use the `humicon` helper for all icons
- Use heroicons as the base icon set
- Custom icons go in app/assets/images/icons
- Icon helper parameters: name (required), class, size, aria
- Example: `= humicon 'trash', class: 'h-4 w-4', aria: { label: 'Delete' }`

##### Styling
- Use TailwindCSS exclusively
- Document any custom classes in Tailwind config
- Avoid inline styles
- Follow Tailwind's utility-first approach
- Group related utilities with @apply when repeated

#### Component Architecture

##### Organization
- Use View Components over partials
- Location: app/frontend/components
  - Generic: widget/ subdirectory
  - Domain-specific: project-specific subdirectories
- Search for existing components before creating new ones
- Justify new components if none exist

##### Component Structure
- Separate presentation from logic
- Keep components focused and single-purpose
- Use proper naming conventions
- Document complex components
- Include examples in component files

#### Template Guidelines

##### Structure
- Use consistent indentation
- Group related elements
- Keep templates DRY
- Use proper HAML syntax
- Leverage helper methods

##### Presentation Logic
- Keep complex logic in component classes
- Use presenters for view-specific formatting
- Avoid business logic in templates
- Cache expensive operations
- Use proper data loading patterns

### HAML Class Attributes Rules
- Never use arbitrary values (square brackets) directly in HAML class attributes
- For Tailwind classes, use one of these two approaches:
  1. Define custom classes in Tailwind config and reference them directly:
     ```haml
     .grid.grid-cols-service-provider
     ```
  2. Use HAML's attribute syntax with curly braces for dynamic classes:
     ```haml
     .grid{ class: "grid-cols-[#{dynamic_value}]" }
     ```
- When combining multiple classes, use HAML's class attribute syntax:
  ```haml
  .base-class{ class: "additional-class" }
  ```

#### Examples

✅ Correct Component:
```ruby
# app/components/widget/button_component.rb
class Widget::ButtonComponent < ViewComponent::Base
  def initialize(label:, variant: :primary)
    @label = label
    @variant = variant
  end
end
```

✅ Correct Template:
```haml
.flex.items-center.gap-2
  = humicon 'check', class: 'h-4 w-4'
  = label
```

❌ Incorrect:
- Raw SVG usage
- Inline styles
- Business logic in templates
- Duplicated components
- Mixed concerns

